@meta
id: 31-06
title: nnoremap becomes vim.keymap.set
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: lua-config
par: 195
minutes: 8
vscode: keybindings.json, one object per binding
open: init.lua

@brief
A mapping in Lua is a function call with four arguments: the mode, the
left-hand side, the right-hand side, and a table of options.

    nnoremap <leader>h :nohlsearch<CR>

becomes

    vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })

Three differences from the vimscript, all of them improvements:

- There is no `nore` variant to remember. `vim.keymap.set` is
  non-recursive by default, which is what you wanted every time anyway.
  Recursive mapping is opt-in with `{ remap = true }`.
- The right-hand side can be a Lua function instead of a string of keys.
  `vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>d', vim.diagnostic.open_float)` maps a key
  straight to a function — no `:call`, no `<Cmd>`, no quoting.
- `{ desc = '...' }` is a human-readable description. It is optional and
  you should write it every time: it is what `:map` shows, what
  `:checkhealth` reports, and what a which-key popup reads. A keymap
  without a `desc` is a keymap nobody can discover six months later.

Leader still works the same way and is still read at definition time, so
`vim.g.mapleader` has to be set before any mapping that uses it — same
trap as chapter 28, new spelling.

Port these two mappings into the file, in this order, with exactly these
descriptions:

    nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>              desc: Write the file
    nnoremap <leader>b :ls<CR>:b<Space>    desc: List buffers and wait

Graded in vim. The Lua is written, not run.

@keys
| `vim.keymap.set(mode, lhs, rhs, opts)` | the whole API |
| `{ desc = 'text' }` | the options table |
| `yyp` | copy a line and put the copy below it |

@hints
Both lines go under the mapping that is already there, and every one of
them has the same five-part shape.
--
`<CR>` and `<Space>` are typed literally inside the quotes, exactly as
they were in the vimrc.
--
You do not have to type the whole thing twice. `yyp` on the existing
mapping gives you a copy to edit, and `ci'` changes one quoted field at a
time.

@solution
Typed straight through, which is what par assumes:

    G o
    vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>w', ':w<CR>', { desc = 'Write the file' })<Enter>
    vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>b', ':ls<CR>:b<Space>', { desc = 'List buffers and wait' })<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

The route a fluent player actually takes is cheaper: `G` `yy` `p` to copy
the existing mapping, then `f'` `;` to hop between the quoted fields and
`ci'` to replace each one. Same file, about half the keystrokes, and no
chance of a typo in the boilerplate. That is worth internalising, because
a real keymap section is thirty near-identical lines and typing them out
is how you end up with `'n'` spelled `'n '` on line nineteen.

One more thing you get for free. Because the third argument can be a
function, a mapping can do real work without a helper command:

    vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>s', function()
      vim.opt.spell = not vim.opt.spell:get()
    end, { desc = 'Toggle spell check' })

In vimscript that is a function definition, a `call`, and a bang you will
forget. This is the single biggest day-to-day reason people move their
config to Lua, and it has nothing to do with speed.

@start init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

vim.g.mapleader = ' '

-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })

@expected init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

vim.g.mapleader = ' '

-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>w', ':w<CR>', { desc = 'Write the file' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>b', ':ls<CR>:b<Space>', { desc = 'List buffers and wait' })
